# {{lb|en|demonstrative}} The thing, person, idea, quality, event, action{{,}} or time indicated or understood from context, especially if more remote geographically, temporally or mentally than one designated as "{{l|en|this}}", or if expressing distinction. {{defdate|from 9<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''That'''{{'}}s my car over there.}}
#: {{ux|en|He went home, and after '''that''' I never saw him again.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet Q1-2|version=Q2|III|i|sig=G2|page=54|text={{w|To be, or not to be}}, '''that''' is the queſtion, / Whether tis nobler in the minde to ſuffer / The ſlings and arrowes of outragious fortune, / Or to take Armes againſt a ſea of troubles, / And by oppoſing, end them, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1888|month=July|journal=The Original Secession Magazine|page=766|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/originalsecessi03unkngoog/page/766/mode/1up
|passage=[A] second man—{{nb...|nospace=1}}—was qualified and fitted, both intellectually and morally,—and '''that''' to an exceptional extent—to be the Head {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Marshall Squire's Daughter|In the Bay of Biscay|16
|passage="I was dragged up at the workhouse school till I was twelve. Then I ran away and sold papers in the streets, and anything else that I could pick up a few coppers by—except steal. I never did '''that'''. I always made up my mind I'd be a big man some day, and—I'm glad I didn't steal."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=w:Peter Hopkirk|title=[[w:The Great Game (Hopkirk book)|The Great Game]]|page=310|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2010
|passage=However{{nb...}}, the British were unable to do much about it short of going to war with St Petersburg, and '''that''' the government was unwilling to do.}}
#* '''2005''', {{w|Joey Comeau}}, ''Lockpick Pornography'' (Loose Teeth Press):
#*: I've never seen someone beaten unconscious before. '''That'''’s lesbians for you.
## {{n-g|Used to refer to a statement just made.}}
##: {{ux|en|They're getting divorced. What do you think about '''''that'''''?}}
## {{non-gloss definition|Used to emphatically affirm or deny a previous statement or question.}}
##: {{ux|en|The water is so cold! — '''That''' it is.}}
##: {{ux|en|Would you like another piece of cake?  — '''That''' I would!}}
##: {{ux|en|We think that you stole the tarts. — '''That''' I did not!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|author=Helen Granville-Barker|title=An Apprentice to Truth|page=214|text="She is very honourable," said Mrs. Thompson, solemnly. "Yes, one sees she is '''that''', and so simple-minded."}}
# {{lb|en|relative|plural '''''that'''''}} {{n-g|In a [[relative clause]], referring to a previously mentioned noun, as subject, direct object, indirect object, or object of a preposition}}; [[which]], [[who]]. {{defdate|from 9<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I didn't see the car '''that''' hit me.}}
#: {{ux|en|The CPR course '''that''' she took really came in handy.}}
#: {{ux|en|The house '''that''' he lived in was old and dilapidated.}}
#: {{ux|en|The poor cat, '''that''' had been trapped for three days, was freed this morning. ''([[non-restrictive relative clause|non-restrictive]] use; sometimes proscribed; see usage notes)''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet Q1-2|I|iv|sig=C3|verso=1|page=19|text=By heauen ile make a ghoſt of him '''that''' lets me, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Telegraph|date=November 11, 2011|author=Jeremy Wilson|title=England Under 21 5 Iceland Under 21 0: match report|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111111144806/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/england/8882713/England-Under-21-5-Iceland-Under-21-0-match-report.html
|passage=His &#91;{{w|Alex Oxlade-Chamberlain}}’s&#93; ability to run at defences is instantly striking, but it is his clever use of possession '''that''' has persuaded some shrewd judges that he is an even better prospect than {{w|Theo Walcott}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845|title=Welcome to the plastisphere|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130719153142/http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21581981-what-pollution-some-opportunity-others-welcome-plastisphere|section=Science and technology
|passage=Plastics are energy-rich substances, which is why many of them burn so readily. Any organism '''that''' could unlock and use that energy would do well in the Anthropocene. Terrestrial bacteria and fungi which can manage this trick are already familiar to experts in the field.}}
